meta {
  name: Reassign Lead
  type: http
  seq: 17
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads/{{disposableLeadId}}/assignment
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{leadReassignToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "producerId": "{{leadReassignUserId}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the new owner, resolved to a name", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("id");
    expect(body).to.have.property("producerId");
    expect(body).to.have.property("producerName");
    expect(body.producerName).to.not.equal("");
  });

  test("bumps lastActivityAt — the Leads list sorts on it", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.have.property("lastActivityAt");
  });

  test("moved the lead to the requested owner", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().producerId).to.equal(bru.getVar("leadReassignUserId"));
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /leads/:id/assignment — hand a lead to another user (PAC-72 D)

  Moves `leads.producerId`, and appends a `lead_reassigned` entry to the lead's
  timeline. That is the **whole** operation — see "No cascade" below.

  - **Module:** `leads`
  - **Permissions:** `leads:write` **AND** `agency:users:read` — both required
  - **Data scope:** the caller must be able to reach the lead
    (`LeadAccessService.loadOwnedLead`, which 404s rather than 403s)

  ## 🔴 Why two permissions

  `agency:users:read` is held by the **Agency Owner and Branch Manager only**.
  A Producer holds `leads:write` but not this, so a producer **cannot reassign
  a lead — not even their own**. Reassignment is an owner/manager action.

  This supersedes the ticket's original "gated on `leads:write`" line (decided
  2026-08-21). It also means the picker's roster — `GET /users`, gated on the
  same permission — is reachable by exactly the people who can act on it, so no
  extra endpoint was needed.

  `@RequirePermissions` is an AND-set in `PermissionsGuard`; the handler spells
  both out rather than using `@RequireWrite`, which sets the same metadata key
  with a single permission.

  ## 🔴 The sold-lead freeze

  A lead can be reassigned **only before it is sold**. Once sold,
  `Deal.producerId` is what the scorecards and the leaderboard read; letting the
  lead move afterwards would make the two disagree about the same sale.
  A sold lead returns **`409`**.

  ⚠ **Known gap, deliberately unfixed.** `PATCH /leads/:id` accepts `status`, so
  a caller holding `leads:write` can flip a lead out of `Sold`, reassign it, and
  flip it back — keying the freeze on status alone is not airtight. The robust
  fix, when wanted, is to also check for a linked `Deal`: a deal existing is a
  fact, a status is an opinion. Recorded so it is a known gap, not a surprise.

  ## No cascade

  This looks larger than it is. Every other record linked to a lead is
  **post-sale** — `deals`, `dealAuditItems`, `priorInsurance` — and therefore
  unreachable behind the freeze.

  `quoteRecaps` *are* reachable and deliberately **do not move**: the original
  quoter keeps the credit and the new owner writes a fresh recap, which is what
  keeps the Quoted scorecard historically accurate.

  ## Body
  | field      | type   | rules                        |
  |------------|--------|------------------------------|
  | producerId | string | required, 24-char hex user id |

  The target must exist, be **active**, and be in the caller's agency.
  Assigning to a deactivated account is refused (`409`) — that is how a lead
  quietly reaches nobody.

  Re-selecting the current owner is a **no-op**: it returns `200` and writes no
  timeline entry, rather than logging a change that did not happen.

  > ⚠ This request targets **Create Disposable Lead**, not `{{createdLeadId}}`.
  > Quote Recaps and Sold Deals both run later *as the producer* and use that
  > lead — moving it to the owner would 404 every one of them. See that
  > request's docs.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "id": "665f...a01",
    "producerId": "665f...b11",
    "producerName": "Pat Producer",
    "lastActivityAt": "2026-08-24T10:12:00.000Z"
  }
  ```

  ## The activity row
  Type `lead_reassigned`, in `ACTIVITY_TYPES` only — never
  `LOGGABLE_ACTIVITY_TYPES`, so no client can forge one.

  > 🔴 `activities.userId` is the **actor**, not the new owner, and is never
  > rewritten. PAC-65 #9 renamed that field from `producerId` precisely because
  > it never meant "owner" — rewriting it so a new producer appears to have made
  > the old one's calls is a data-integrity bug, not a feature.

  Both names go in `summary`, deliberately **not** in `changes`: a
  `field_changed` row is hidden from anyone without
  `agency:changelogs:read` — which is every producer — and the producer losing
  the lead is precisely who needs to see it.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — missing or malformed `producerId`.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing `leads:write` or `agency:users:read`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such lead (or out of scope), or no such user.
  - `409 Conflict` — the lead is sold, or the target user is deactivated.
}
